      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>The Framework of Pay Revisions in Uttar Pradesh</h2>
        <p>
          As India's most populous state, <strong>Uttar Pradesh</strong> maintains one of the largest public sector workforces in the country. With more than sixteen lakh active state employees—spanning the Uttar Pradesh Secretariat (Sachivalaya), District Collectorates, UP Police Department, Basic Shiksha Parishad primary schools, Secondary Education colleges, and Revenue field staff—alongside over twelve lakh pensioners, the recommendations of the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission (8th CPC)</strong> carry immense socio-economic significance.
        </p>
        <p>
          Historically, the Government of Uttar Pradesh does not maintain a permanent, standalone pay commission that operates independently of the Union Government. Instead, upon notification of a Central Pay Commission by the Ministry of Finance, the Chief Minister constitutes a high-level <strong>State Pay Committee (Vetan Samiti)</strong> headed by a retired senior civil servant or judge, supported by the Principal Secretary (Finance).
        </p>
        <p>
          The mandate of the UP Vetan Samiti is to review central recommendations, evaluate state revenue receipts versus committed expenditures, and issue comprehensive Government Orders (<em>Shasanadesh</em>) extending parity to state cadres. Under the 7th CPC revision, Uttar Pradesh achieved near-total pay matrix parity with the Centre, establishing a synchronized foundation for the 8th Pay Commission.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Key Administrative Cadres in UP: Pay Levels and Roles</h2>
        <p>
          The Uttar Pradesh public service framework covers several distinct departmental hierarchies:
        </p>

        <div class="cadre-card-grid">
